 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "eq:4.3.12",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "expanding-the-wavefunction",
   "prompt": "<p>A particle in a box of length $L$ is in the state $\\Psi = \\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{3}}\\,\\psi_1 + \\sqrt{\\tfrac{2}{3}}\\,\\psi_2$, where $\\psi_1$ and $\\psi_2$ are the box's two lowest normalized eigenstates of $\\hat{H}$, with energies $E_1$ and $E_2 = 4E_1$.</p><ol type='a'><li>What is the probability that a measurement of the energy gives $E_1$?</li><li>What is the probability that it gives $E_2$?</li><li>What is the expectation value $\\langle E\\rangle$, in units of $E_1$?</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 0.3333
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 0.6667
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": 3,
      "unit": "E₁"
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>The weights of the expansion are $c_1 = \\sqrt{1/3}$ and $c_2 = \\sqrt{2/3}$. A measurement of the energy gives an eigenvalue of $\\hat{H}$, $E_1$ with probability $|c_1|^2 = 1/3$ and $E_2$ with probability $|c_2|^2 = 2/3$. These add to 1, as $\\sum_i |c_i|^2 = 1$ requires for a normalized state.</p><p>The expectation value weights each energy by its probability:</p>$$ \\langle E\\rangle = \\int \\Psi^\\ast \\hat{H}\\Psi\\,dx = |c_1|^2E_1 + |c_2|^2E_2 = \\tfrac{1}{3}E_1 + \\tfrac{2}{3}(4E_1) = 3E_1 $$<p>The cross terms vanish because $\\psi_1$ and $\\psi_2$ are orthogonal. No single measurement gives $3E_1$; it is the mean of many.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "eq:4.3.9",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "eigenvalue-equations",
   "prompt": "<p>Show that $\\psi(x) = Ne^{ikx}$ is an eigenstate of the momentum operator $\\hat{p}_x = -i\\hbar\\,d/dx$, and that the variance of $p_x$ in this state is zero.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Apply $\\hat{p}_x$ once and then twice, and use $\\sigma^2 = \\langle p_x^2\\rangle - \\langle p_x\\rangle^2$.",
    "solution": "<p>Acting with the momentum operator,</p>$$ \\hat{p}_x\\psi = -i\\hbar\\dfrac{d}{dx}Ne^{ikx} = -i\\hbar(ik)Ne^{ikx} = \\hbar k\\,\\psi $$<p>so $\\psi$ is an eigenstate with eigenvalue $\\hbar k$. Applying the operator twice gives $\\hat{p}_x^2\\psi = (\\hbar k)^2\\psi$. With $\\psi$ normalized over the region considered, $\\langle p_x\\rangle = \\hbar k\\int\\psi^\\ast\\psi\\,dx = \\hbar k$ and $\\langle p_x^2\\rangle = (\\hbar k)^2$, so</p>$$ \\sigma_{p}^2 = \\langle p_x^2\\rangle - \\langle p_x\\rangle^2 = (\\hbar k)^2 - (\\hbar k)^2 = 0 $$<p>Every measurement of the momentum gives $\\hbar k$. The same state has a flat probability density $N^2$, so its position is not defined at all, as Section 4.1 found.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
